#!/bin/bash
# 703 (Sat 21 Nov 2026 15:57, Peter: "why can't you test 3, 4 and 5 now? This is just waiting ... did the goal signal go back on
# and can it predict a goal yet? That should be used in conjunction"). Starts NOW, alongside whatever else is running, in its own
# folder so it does not wait: bookmaker prices against the exchange (history today, live recording from today), and the goal
# signal put through an honest test. Paper only. No orders. No accounts opened. Nothing deleted.
S=/root/pe-sports/books; mkdir -p $S /root/pe-builder/runs
cat > /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_books.md <<'BRIEF'
You are a Builder on Peter Edwards' trading server, session 11, started to run ALONGSIDE other sessions. Work only in /root/pe-sports/books (new files) and new tables in schema 'books'; read anything, but do not edit files in /root/pe-sports/grid or the paper trader. HARD RULES: paper only; never an order endpoint; never log in to, open or touch any bookmaker or exchange betting account; nothing deleted; credentials only through /root/pe-queue/runner/creds.py, never printed; no new sign-ups, no purchases, no new API keys - use only feeds this server already has. Stay inside the API-Football plan's daily calls (leave at least 40,000 a day for the collectors already running). No idle loops; collectors are plain timers or sleep loops registered in /root/pe-mechanic/watch.list.
WHY: about 9,600 rules that take a price on the exchange lose the cost of trading. Peter's question: why are we not being FASTER, and why are the other routes not tested now. We cannot beat the exchange to a goal (our feeds are 1-3 minutes behind and Betfair delays in-play bets). But the exchange itself is the fastest, sharpest price there is, and we record it tick by tick. Ordinary bookmakers move later. So be faster than THEM.
PART A - BOOKMAKERS AGAINST THE SHARP PRICE, ON HISTORY, TODAY. Find on this server every source of bookmaker prices: the football-data files (opening and closing prices for bet365, William Hill, Pinnacle and others, market maximum and average, Betfair Exchange where present), anything API-Football has stored, anything else (list them with dates and rows). Then test, on all seasons held, first half of seasons to find and second half to check, always from information available BEFORE the bet: back a selection at a named bookmaker when its price beats the fair price (the sharp price with its margin removed - Pinnacle closing, and Betfair Exchange after 2% commission where held) by 2%, 3%, 5%, 8%, 10%; by league, market (match result, over/under, Asian handicap where held), price band (Peter prefers backs and dislikes long shots: show under 3.0, 3-5, 5+ separately), opening against closing prices. Report per bookmaker: bets a week, £ kept per £100 on each half, the worst season, and how often the same bet was available at two or more bookmakers. Be straight about the two catches: closing prices are known only at the close (so also test opening bookmaker price against the sharp price at the SAME time where the files allow, and say which result is usable in real life), and bookmakers limit accounts that win.
PART B - RECORD BOOKMAKER PRICES LIVE, FROM TODAY. Using API-Football's odds data already paid for (pre-match odds by bookmaker, and in-play odds if the plan gives them): a collector for every fixture we also record on Betfair - each bookmaker's price for match result, over/under 2.5, both teams to score, stamped with the time we received it - every 10 minutes from 3 hours before kick-off and every 2 minutes in the last 30 (inside the call budget; say what cadence the budget allows). Join to the Betfair prices of the same second (reuse the checked team-name links in /root/pe-sports/grid/cache/fact_links.csv and team_alias.csv; never guess a link). Table books.gaps: every moment a bookmaker's back price is higher than the Betfair LAY price by enough to lock a profit after 2% commission (an arbitrage), and every moment it beats the Betfair fair price by 3%+ (a value bet); how long each lasted; which bookmaker; how stale that bookmaker's price was. First numbers tonight from this evening's matches: how many, which bookmakers lag most, by how many minutes, £ locked per £100 if taken. Also say how late API-Football's copy of a bookmaker's price is compared with the bookmaker's own site if that can be measured (if the feed is minutes old the gaps may already be gone - say so).
PART C - THE GOAL SIGNAL, HONESTLY. The goal monitor (/root/pe-brain/repo/brain/monitor_ht.py, pattern_matcher.py; table goal_alerts) is recording quietly; emails are off. Its live inputs were restarted today. Answer three things with numbers: (1) is it running and alerting on today's matches (count, last alert time); (2) can it predict a goal: over every alert ever stored that can be joined to a match result and goal times - goals in the 10, 15 and 20 minutes after an alert against the same minutes of comparable matches with no alert (same minute of play, same score state, same league tier) - the uplift, with first half and second half of the dates, and whether the uplift survives after allowing for the delay of its inputs; (3) is the uplift already in the price: at each alert, the Betfair over/under and next-goal-relevant prices at the alert moment (plus the measured input delay), and the result of BACKING the goal side (over the next line, back side first) with a target, a stop and a time limit, through the same fill rules as the one test (take the price on offer, money shown, 2% commission) - £ kept per £100, both halves. Then 'in conjunction', as Peter asked: does an alert improve any of today's other candidates - a quoter that stops offering unders / offers overs when an alert is live; a bookmaker's over price left stale after an alert. One line each.
WRITE /root/pe-queue/reports/books_and_goal_signal.md, plain English, one screen, answer first: line 1 = bookmakers against the sharp price on history: £ kept per £100 on the unseen half, bets a week, which bookmakers; line 2 = live gaps seen tonight: how many, how long they last, which bookmakers; line 3 = goal signal: running yes/no, predicts goals yes/no with the uplift, beats the price yes/no with £ per £100. Then what is needed to act on each (for bookmakers: accounts, which Peter must say he has), then the tables. Push a first version within 90 minutes even if Part B has only a few matches, and again at the end: cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/books_and_goal_signal.md && git commit -qm "bookmakers against the exchange; goal signal test" && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q. Leave the Part B collector running. Then end the session.
BRIEF
cat > /root/pe-builder/run_books.sh <<'SH'
#!/bin/bash
CL=$(cat /root/pe-watchman/claude_path); ts=$(date -u +%F_%H%M)
cd /root/pe-sports/books && timeout -k 60 21600 "$CL" -p "$(cat /root/pe-builder/BRIEF_books.md)" --allowedTools "Bash,Read,Write,Edit,Glob,Grep" --max-turns 600 --output-format text > /root/pe-builder/runs/${ts}_books.log 2>&1
echo "books session ended $(date -u '+%F %H:%M') exit $?" >> /root/pe-builder/sessions.log
SH
chmod +x /root/pe-builder/run_books.sh
nohup setsid /root/pe-builder/run_books.sh > /dev/null 2>&1 < /dev/null & sleep 20
echo "books session: $(pgrep -fc '[r]un_books.sh') | builders: $(pgrep -fa '[r]un_builder' | grep -o 'run_builder[a-z_]*' | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ') | $(date -u +%H:%M) UTC" > /root/pe-queue/reports/running_now.md
cd /root/pe-queue && git add reports/running_now.md && git commit -qm "running now" -q && git pull -q --no-rebase --no-edit && env -u GITHUB_TOKEN git push -q
